llm_response:
Imitrex (sumatriptan) is a triptan-class medication indicated for the acute (abortive) treatment of migraine attacks, and it has demonstrated effectiveness for a substantial proportion of patients, though not all respond. ;

Educational and clinical overviews describe triptans, including sumatriptan (Imitrex), as the “gold standard” for acute migraine therapy, with oral triptans effective in approximately 29% to 64% of patients at two hours depending on outcome criteria, and they are recommended to be taken as early as possible in an attack [0, 1]. Mechanistically, sumatriptan acts as a selective 5‑HT1B/1D receptor agonist that leads to cranial vasoconstriction and modulation of trigeminal pathways, which can relieve migraine pain, although response varies among individuals [1]. ;

Evidence reviews note that real‑world effectiveness is mixed: about 50% of users may experience significant improvement, and up to 30% may have only partial or no relief, indicating that it works for many but not all patients [0, 1]. Safety considerations include common side effects such as nausea and dizziness and potential cardiovascular symptoms; triptans are contraindicated in patients with coronary artery disease or uncontrolled hypertension due to their vasoconstrictive effects [0]. ;

Given variability in response and tolerability, clinicians often individualize therapy and may consider alternatives (e.g., other triptans, DHE/ergots when appropriate, CGRP‑pathway options, or nonpharmacologic strategies) if sumatriptan is ineffective or not tolerated [0, 1]. ;

In summary, it is not accurate to say that Imitrex cannot treat acute migraine attacks; it is an established abortive therapy that helps many patients, though a significant minority do not respond or cannot use it due to side effects or contraindications [0, 1]. ; [0, 1]
